WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.783
Grafana is a web app for looking at data that lives somewhere else.

2
00:00:06.783 --> 00:00:10.073
In the lab, Prometheus scrapes and stores the metrics.

3
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.960
Grafana asks Prometheus questions in PromQL, and draws the answers.

4
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.342
Its own small database holds dashboards, users and alert rules.

5
00:00:19.342 --> 00:00:21.499
The numbers stay in Prometheus.

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.700
Each system Grafana can ask is a data source.

7
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:33.095
One Grafana can talk to many at once: Prometheus for metrics, Loki for logs, Tempo for traces, and more.

8
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.796
Here is what happens when you open a dashboard.

9
00:00:36.796 --> 00:00:40.875
Your browser loads the dashboard's JSON from the Grafana server.

10
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.306
For each panel, the browser sends the panel's query to Grafana.

11
00:00:45.306 --> 00:00:47.643
Grafana passes it on to Prometheus.

12
00:00:47.643 --> 00:00:51.381
That is what access proxy means in the data source file.

13
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.212
Prometheus answers, and the browser draws the result.

14
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:59.179
So Grafana can't show anything Prometheus never scraped.

15
00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:02.657
An empty graph is usually a scrape or query problem.

16
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:08.069
A panel is one box on a dashboard: a query, and a visualization.

17
00:01:08.069 --> 00:01:10.371
This one is a time series graph.

18
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.070
A graph panel sends a range query.

19
00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:18.592
Prometheus runs the same PromQL once for each point, at evenly spaced times.

20
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.045
The gap between points is the step.

21
00:01:21.045 --> 00:01:24.829
Grafana picks it from the time range and the panel's width.

22
00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:28.228
On the lab's 30 minute graph, the step is 15 seconds.

23
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:32.728
Stretch the range to 7 days, and the points are many minutes apart.

24
00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:35.474
Say there are short spikes between the points.

25
00:01:35.474 --> 00:01:40.776
A one minute window then looks at one minute out of every ten, and misses the spikes in between.

26
00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:44.388
$__rate_interval grows with the step.

27
00:01:44.388 --> 00:01:50.751
Grafana takes the larger of two values: the step plus one scrape interval, or four scrape intervals.

28
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:55.129
That only works if Grafana knows the scrape interval.

29
00:01:55.129 --> 00:02:01.792
That is timeInterval 15s in the data source file, and it must match scrape_interval in prometheus.yml.

30
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.252
On the 30 minute graph, the range became one minute: four scrapes.

31
00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:12.435
What comes back is a set of series: one per status, each a list of values over time.

32
00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:17.139
Before any request has failed, the 500 series does not exist at all.

33
00:02:17.139 --> 00:02:19.507
Here Redis was stopped near the end.

34
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:22.215
The panel draws each series as a line.

35
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:30.440
Write {{status}} in the legend field, and each line is named from its labels: 200, or 500.

36
00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:33.318
Settings change only the display.

37
00:02:33.318 --> 00:02:42.797
The unit turns 4.42 into 4.42 requests per second, and an override paints the 500 line red, whatever the palette says.

38
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:48.079
A dashboard variable is a dropdown at the top of the dashboard.

39
00:02:48.079 --> 00:02:51.175
Its value goes into every query that mentions it.

40
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.143
job is a Query variable.

41
00:02:53.143 --> 00:02:57.282
Grafana asks Prometheus for every value of the job label.

42
00:02:57.282 --> 00:02:59.606
Today that is only hello-devops.

43
00:02:59.606 --> 00:03:03.672
With 40 services, the same dashboard would serve all 40.

44
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:05.624
path depends on job.

45
00:03:05.624 --> 00:03:11.183
It lists the paths of the chosen job, allows several at once, and has an All option.

46
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:18.020
With several values selected, Grafana inserts a regular expression, slash, pipe, slash healthz.

47
00:03:18.233 --> 00:03:22.039
So queries must match with equals tilde, not with equals.

48
00:03:22.039 --> 00:03:25.244
Forgetting this is the most common variable bug.

49
00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:29.362
Underneath, every dashboard is a JSON document.

50
00:03:29.362 --> 00:03:33.568
A uid, the variables, and the panels, each with its queries.

51
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.885
The uid is part of the URL.

52
00:03:35.885 --> 00:03:38.741
Keep it fixed, and links never break.

53
00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:43.907
If the JSON is the dashboard, keep it in git, and review changes like code.

54
00:03:43.907 --> 00:03:49.177
Provisioning loads such files when Grafana starts, from its provisioning folder.

55
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:56.484
In the provider file, allowUiUpdates false makes the Save button refuse, because the file is the truth.

56
00:03:56.484 --> 00:04:00.955
And Grafana checks the folder for changed files every 30 seconds.

57
00:04:01.767 --> 00:04:09.851
So the everyday loop is: change the dashboard in the UI until you like it, export its JSON, and paste it over the file.

58
00:04:09.851 --> 00:04:12.024
Commit, and open a pull request.

59
00:04:12.024 --> 00:04:15.013
Grafana picks up the file within 30 seconds.

60
00:04:15.833 --> 00:04:18.140
Grafana stores dashboards, not data.

61
00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:25.703
Every panel sends its query through a data source, over the time range at a step, and draws the series that come back.
